			<div class="content" id="post-content-4707356">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Drama/Emotional</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A man who at night is sometimes visited by alternate versions of himself who all appear successful and happy with their lives (e.g. doctors, business men, members of the military or people in happy relationships.). These apparitions will tell him about things he could have done better and mistakes he made in his life. Telling him that he could have done so much better.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong>(edited) The man didn't live a good life before going into containment, drinking unhealthy amounts of alcohol and ending up in a dead end job. The combination of being told he wasn't good enough by himself and being locked in containment by the foundation gave him a distaste for authority, since they both reminded him of the versions of himself and prevented him from trying to improve himself and live a better life outside of containment. The man becomes increasingly angry at his apparitions' mocking and becomes less cooperative with the foundation. One day there is a containment breach at his site and he decides to escape in the confusion to try and have another shot at life and leaves a note as sort of middle finger to the foundation.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> (edited) I want the reader to feel for the situation of the man, showing his sadness and regret of his choices, then his frustration at those around him and finally the hopeful but unclear ending for him. Hopefully showing the effects of the apparitions on the man and the anxiety that they represent for him.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> Just thought I'd clarify that the apparitions aren't actually alternate versions of himself, just representations of his anxieties and regrets.</p>

