<p>Hello there,</p>
<p>I can't greenlight, but I will try to review this:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Ok, so I absolutely love this idea at first. A man with a failed life that gets visited by himself every night, stating what he could have done different. This is really engaging, at least for me.</p>
<p>I do think the part he tries to commit suicide is a bit raw in this description as it feels like &quot;He got mad and died&quot;, so maybe develop the story a bit more before getting to that point. Then another copy of him convinces him not to do it, but he is put in suicide watch. This feels like those videogames &quot;bad ending&quot;, which doesn't feel right, and isn't near as compelling and fun as the &quot;good ending&quot;, which I think you should try to do.</p>
<p>My suggestion for a good ending would be to have his copies not compare his failed life to their successful one anymore after he tried suicide, but yet start to develop a friendship with the SCP, convince him that, even though he made bad choices in his life, he could still turn it around.</p>
<p>Another thing for the narrative: Usual SCP articles are the point of view of the researchers into the SCP's abnormalities, but maybe, you could make this one a diary that is found with the SCP telling the story you told on the narrative together with what I've suggested, and the narrative are the researchers investigating the mental state of the man, and what causes his visions.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Best of luck!</p>

