<p>Thanks for your crit, if you are looking for ways to make the character feel less generic, he does gain a disdain for authority since many of the visions that he sees are in positions of authority and make him feel inferior.</p>
<p>As for the ending, the inclusion of the suicide in the narrative is because he is made to feel hopeless about his situation by his visions. He's told that all his life choices were mistakes and is constantly reminded that he is stuck in containment for the rest of his life, but I understand why it may not be the best idea since it is very sensitive as a topic. The alternative option that you suggested in dms of him escaping does seem like a interesting idea, he could decide that he's had enough of being kept in containment and being stopped from achieving his full potential by the foundation. He could decide that he should try and escape during a containment breach to try and make his life what it could have been, it does seem like a good way to end his story.</p>
<p>I'm honestly quite torn between these two options, both seem like good potential ending for his story. Do you think there is one of the ideas that I should pursue over the other?</p>

