<blockquote>
<p>but I understand why it may not be the best idea since it is very sensitive as a topic.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's not even just that, part of my issue is that it's a rather obvious conclusion to his story and not one that feels like it fits with his character arc as well as I'd like to see.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>he could decide that he's had enough of being kept in containment and being stopped from achieving his full potential by the foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is particularly why I like this. It's a more thematically relevant option. If his story ends with suicide, the meaning of that is all present on the textual level (in my opinion, at least), but with this, his escape can represent a level of character development. Specifically, one that reflects a newfound belief in his own value, and a commitment to pursue self-improvement.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Do you think there is one of the ideas that I should pursue over the other?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Perhaps get input from other reviewers on this, but I think the option with him escaping is the stronger one in terms of character and thematic relevance.</p>

