<blockquote>
<p>One day there is a containment breach at his site and he decides to escape in the confusion to try and have another shot at life and leaves a note as sort of middle finger to the foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A random containment breach doesn't feel like the necessary payoff here. This guy doesn't have to <em>do</em> anything or change at all for this series of events to happen, so it feels very disconnected from his character arc. What would be much stronger here, in my opinion, is external action from him that demonstrates his internal character development. Perhaps he's planned an escape with these apparitions, or developed a relationship with someone in the Foundation, or learned a skill that lets him escape or something else that demonstrates his change as a person.</p>
<p>I think you also need a more clearly defined character arc and a clearer sense of what events will trigger major changes in his character. He starts depressed, and he ends believing himself worthy of second chance, but what happens in between those two states? What moves him from that first feeling into the second? This is really built around his character so this is the most important element of the narrative to nail down.</p>

