			<div class="content" id="post-content-4719688">
									

<p>Hey there! I hope you're doing alright!<br />
Let's take a look at your concept, shall we?</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Here's&nbsp;OUR&nbsp;critique,&nbsp;fellow&nbsp;Comrade</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>It's a concept that has its ups and downs, and I'm going to help you. First, you're trying to write a Humanoid SCP object. The problem with this type of anomaly is that they're hard to write, especially for new writers. Of what I understood, it is a person that sees several versions of himself with a better life than him. The problem with this is that it isn't anomalous, you too said it. So, why is it contained in the first place? It makes it look like it has hallucinations. How're you going to write the representations? Is it going to be in a journal where he writes his experiences? Do they manifest physically?</p>
<p>It's a good point that you want to make your narrative tragic, but it seems a bit cliché and not developed deeply. Write a reason for falling into addictions. More tragic so the reader can empathize more with him. However, I'm sure the Foundation would be aware of these hallucinations, since they've numerous psychologists and psychiatrists, at least one of them would notice his change in behavior.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One day there is a containment breach at his site and he decides to escape in the confusion to try and have another shot at life and leaves a note as a sort of middle finger to the foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I do like the ending you wrote (it's better than suicide), but I'm sure the Foundation wouldn't doubt twice in capturing the anomaly if it escaped. His liberty wouldn't be that long.</p>
<p>Summarizing, I see potential in this concept, especially the narrative. But there are holes in it, mostly on the anomaly itself. If you want to fix these mistakes, I suggest you read these guides and essays:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/ideation|">Ideation: A Guide To Making Good Ideas Out Of Nothing</a>.</li>
<li><a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/mackenzie-pitfalls|">Dr. Mackenzie's Common SCP Pitfalls</a>.</li>
<li><a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/mackenzie-tips|">Dr. Mackenzie's Documentation Tips</a>.</li>
<li><a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/yourjouney-part-1|">The Journey of Your First SCP: Part I - Introduction &amp; Ideation</a>.</li>
<li><a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/yourjouney-part-2|">The Journey of Your First SCP: Part II - Narratives &amp; Originality</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Also, contact members form the <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/butterfly-squad-roster|">Butterfly Squad Roaster</a> for more reviews on your idea, that it's interesting.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Good luck, Comrade!
