<p>Thank you very much for your critique Comrade.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So, why is it contained in the first place? It makes it look like it has hallucinations.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The manifestations are physical, like intangible ghosts that appear in front of the guy at night, keeping in his line of sight as best they can, following him around, waking him up if he's asleep and disappearing again once they've said what they have to say.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it seems a bit cliché and not developed deeply. Write a reason for falling into addictions. More tragic so the reader can empathize more with him.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Right, I didn't really know how much detail I could have gone into his life in the original post, so I'll just write about it here. The man did very well in school early on, he got good grades and was told that he'd do well in life by his peers. He let that idea that he would sail through life get into his head and stopped working as hard as he could have. This lead to him not getting the grades that he needed to go to a good college or university. This meant he wound up working a job he wasn't happy with and the feeling of regret that he'd wasted his shot. He started drinking alcohol to try and relieve some of his stress and anxiety. His anomaly manifested because of his feelings that he'd wasted his potentially good life.<br />
*As an extra note to his backstory: since his anomaly mainly appears during the night, the foundation only discovered him when he stayed out too late drinking at a pub and a version of himself manifested.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm sure the Foundation wouldn't doubt twice in capturing the anomaly if it escaped. His liberty wouldn't be that long.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I agree with the idea that he would eventually be recaptured. But the point of it isn't necessarily the physical freedom, it's the fact that he's finally trying to do something with his life and stopped feeling sorry about himself. He stopped listening to what his other versions of himself said and decided that he could at least try to do something with his life.</p>
<p>Thanks again for the critique, I hope that I've clarified a few misconceptions about the idea.</p>

