<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Placeholder! Got summoned via PM to take a look-see. Will crit in a bit.</span></p>
<p>Alright, here’s my take.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>At the moment, most of my views on this idea have been expressed quite eloquently by <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ocuin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6104981); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6104981&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1595133289" alt="OCuin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6104981)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ocuin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6104981); return false;">OCuin</a></span>. The basic premise of your anomaly certainly holds potential, but it’s weighed down by a lack of pay-off at the end of your protagonist’s emotional arc. A convenient containment breach a certain point along this arc doesn’t make up for that.</p>
<p>Allow me to put it another way. If your character gets more and more resentful of authority and grows more and more discontent, you’re building up narrative tension. If your character disappears with an angry note, that doesn’t relieve the tension so much as it lets it seep away, potentially leaving your reader feeling unsatisfied. What ending could you use to provide your character with a clear ending that will resolve the conflict created by previous animosity towards authority?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
If you respond to this comment and would like me to see it, please send me a Wikidot PM, as I will not be notified otherwise.
