<blockquote>
<p>Why are they trying to improve him at all if they just have it out for him? What is the apparitions' actual motive here?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The apparitions are a manifestation of his anxieties and his low self esteem, like you said they represent better versions of the man that are unattainable, but also these better versions represent points in his life where things could have gone differently if he had acted in a certain way. So by trying to change him in ways that reflect what the different apparitions are they are reminding him of his shortcomings.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I think you'd have to identify what you want the man's emotional state and personality to be at the beginning and the end of the story</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This was one of the reasons I thought about them attempting to change the man. At the beginning he could be incredibly caught up in what they have to say about him and is desperate for their advice, but as the article progresses and he is treated more and more harshly he begins to grow the aversion to authority which eventually leads to him lashing out and arguing with an apparition. He ends being much more independent and believes in himself more than before. I think that an ending like that may show more growth than the escape idea since the escape is more luck than the man's skill or independence and the other ending could stop the tension from seeping away as it would with leaving an angry note.</p>
<p>Your questions have been really helpful for developing this idea more, so your thoughts on this would be good to hear.</p>

